+---
+id: dl-005-createpatch-array-diff-uses-lcs-with-adjacent-delete-insert-merged-into-replace
+type: decision-log
+title: "createPatch array diff uses LCS with adjacent delete+insert merged into replace"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+`createPatch(from, to)` (task-005) must produce a JSON Patch that "describes what changed, and
+nothing more": a request explicitly made to avoid resending a whole document for a small change.
+Arrays are the hard case, with two simpler strategies available and rejected:
+
+- **Whole-array replace** whenever any element differs. Simplest, but defeats the purpose for the
+  common case of a single changed, inserted or removed element in a large array.
+- **Index-wise comparison** (compare `from[i]` to `to[i]` up to the shorter length, `replace` the
+  differing ones, then `add`/`remove` any extra trailing elements). Cheap, but a single insertion or
+  removal before the end shifts every later index, turning the rest of the array into spurious
+  `replace` ops — exactly the "resending everything" problem for a different reason.
+
+A longest-common-subsequence (LCS) edit script, matching elements between `from` and `to` by deep
+equality, localizes the diff to the elements that actually moved relative to the common subsequence,
+regardless of where in the array the change is. Its cost is an O(n·m) DP table (time and space) over
+the two arrays' lengths, which is standard for text/array diffing and acceptable for the document
+sizes this library targets.
+
+Taken alone, LCS has one awkward consequence: an element that is simply changed in place (same index,
+different value) has no common element to match against, so the raw edit script represents it as a
+`delete` of the old value immediately followed by an `insert` of the new one at the same resulting
+index, rather than the more natural single `replace`.
+
+## Decision
+
+- `diffArrays` (in `src/jsonPatch.ts`) builds the LCS edit script (`arrayEditScript`) and then walks
+  it left to right, tracking the current index in the array as ops would apply it.
+- Immediately after producing a `delete` step, if the next step is an `insert`, the two are merged
+  into a single `replace` op at the same index instead of a `remove` followed by an `add`.
+- Everything else from the raw edit script maps directly: an unmerged `delete` is a `remove`, an
+  unmerged `insert` is an `add`, both at the index the walk has reached so far (`match` steps advance
+  the index without emitting an op).
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A single element changed in place produces exactly one `replace` op, matching what a user would
+  expect and what the whole-document-resend requirement rules out.
+- Insertions and removals anywhere in the array produce localized `add`/`remove` ops; unrelated
+  elements before and after are left untouched, even when they shift index position as a result.
+- Runs of several changed elements in a row are paired delete-then-insert in script order, which is
+  not guaranteed to pair each old element with its "intended" replacement when multiple candidates tie
+  in the LCS backtrack; the result is still a correct, minimal-shape patch (verified by the
+  round-trip test in task-005), just not necessarily the most semantically intuitive pairing in that
+  specific edge case. Revisit only if a concrete case shows this producing a confusing patch in
+  practice.
+- The LCS table is O(n·m) time and space; this is a known tradeoff and fine for this library's
+  target document sizes, but `createPatch` should not be used as-is for very large arrays (tens of
+  thousands of elements) without revisiting this.
